.Dd September 17, 2026
.Dt CAPRECLAIM 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm capreclaim_run ,
.Nm capreclaim_fini
.Nd reconcile a provider's per-bundle resources against the live set
.Sh LIBRARY
Capability resource reconcile library
.Pq libcapreclaim, Fl lcapreclaim
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In capreclaim.h
.Ft int
.Fn capreclaim_run "struct capreclaim *r" "enum capreclaim_when when"
.Ft void
.Fn capreclaim_fini "struct capreclaim *r"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A capability-plane provider that holds state on behalf of installed bundles
.Pq storage containers, kernel keys, log records
must release that state once a bundle is neither installed nor running.
.Nm libcapreclaim
owns everything hard about that: reading the
.Em live set ,
computing the orphans, the schedule, and the grace.
The provider supplies only two callbacks and the descriptors of its live-set
sources.
.Pp
The
.Vt struct capreclaim
the caller fills in must be initialised with
.Dv CAPRECLAIM_INIT ,
which zeroes it and stamps
.Va struct_size
with the caller's own
.Fn sizeof ,
then has these fields:
.Bl -tag -width "allow_empty_live"
.It Va struct_size
Set by
.Dv CAPRECLAIM_INIT
to
.Fn sizeof "struct capreclaim"
at the caller's compile time.
It is the ABI-skew guard: a provider built against one version of the library
and dynamically linked against another reports the size of the struct it
actually allocated, so the library never reads an optional field past the end
of an older, smaller struct.
.Fn capreclaim_run
rejects a
.Va struct_size
of zero or one out of range with
.Er EINVAL ,
which also catches a struct that was never initialised, so every reconciler
.Em must
be initialised with
.Dv CAPRECLAIM_INIT .
.It Va sources , Va nsources
Up to four directory descriptors the live set is read from, each with a
.Va strip_cap
flag saying whether entries are named
.Pa <owner>.cap
.Pq the install folders
or
.Pa <owner>
.Pq the runtime markers .
A source with descriptor \-1 is skipped.
The live set is their union: an owner listed by any source is live.
Reading is a plain
.Xr readdir 3 ,
so a sandboxed provider passes the descriptors delivered to it and never
opens a path.
.It Va enumerate
Called with an
.Fa emit
function; the provider emits every owner it currently holds resources for.
.It Va destroy
Called once per orphan to free that owner's resources; return \-1 with
.Va errno
on failure.
A failure is counted and the owner is retried on the next pass.
.It Va arg
Passed to both callbacks.
.It Va stats
Optional; when non-NULL, filled with the pass's counts
.Pq live, owned, orphans, destroyed, failed
for the caller's log line or DTrace probe.
.It Va allow_empty_live
Opt out of the empty-live-set floor, described below.
.El
.Pp
.Fn capreclaim_run
performs one pass.
With
.Dv CAPRECLAIM_BOOT
the state is settled
.Pq no package operation is running yet
and every orphan is destroyed immediately.
With
.Dv CAPRECLAIM_TIMER
an orphan is destroyed only if it was also orphaned on the previous timer
pass
.Pq seen gone twice ,
so the interval between passes is the grace window: a bundle's transient
absence during an upgrade is never confirmed.
The function returns the number of owners destroyed, or \-1 with
.Va errno
when the live set could not be read at all
.Pq no source was readable ,
in which case nothing is destroyed.
.Pp
Safety floors: a pass that read at least one source but found the live set
.Em empty
destroys nothing, because an empty set almost always means the sources have
not been published yet, not that everything was uninstalled.
A provider whose sources are gated on their own readiness signal
.Pq a marker directory the publisher creates before anything runs
may set
.Va allow_empty_live
to treat an empty set as genuinely empty; it is honoured only when at least one
source was actually read.
Owner names are validated as single safe path components before
.Va destroy
is called.
.Pp
.Fn capreclaim_fini
releases the grace state kept between timer passes.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn capreclaim_run
returns the number of owners destroyed on this pass, or \-1 and sets
.Va errno
if no live-set source could be read.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A provider's reconcile loop, run in a child forked before the provider enters
capability mode, with the install folders and the run markers delivered as
descriptors:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct capreclaim r = {
	.sources = {
		{ .fd = system_fd, .strip_cap = true },
		{ .fd = apps_fd,   .strip_cap = true },
		{ .fd = runlive_fd, .strip_cap = false },
	},
	.nsources = 3,
	.enumerate = my_enumerate,
	.destroy = my_destroy,
	.arg = state,
	.stats = &stats,
};
enum capreclaim_when when = CAPRECLAIM_BOOT;

for (;;) {
	int n = capreclaim_run(&r, when);
	if (n >= 0)
		when = CAPRECLAIM_TIMER;
	sleep(when == CAPRECLAIM_BOOT ? 3 : interval);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr service_storage_open 3 ,
.Xr localcrypto 8 ,
.Xr logd 8 ,
.Xr switchboard 8 ,
.Xr tzfsd 8
.Pp
.Pa docs/capability-container-model.md
in the source tree describes the container model this library implements.
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm libcapreclaim
first appeared with the capability container model, replacing the
installation ledger and its per-provider reclaim protocol.
